/-! # TIPD118: an OPA1611 attenuator driving its output capacitor, certified from netlist text

A pilot for certifying an op-amp stage that drives a capacitive load (#8983, milestones 1–3):
TI's reference design TIPD118, the band-pass filtered, inverting −40 dB attenuator of the design
guide SLAU518 (June 2013), with a linear macromodel of the OPA1611 built from netlist elements.
Every certificate below was produced by an untrusted exact generator and is checked by the kernel
(`decide +kernel`) against the netlist text.

## The circuit (SLAU518, checked against its schematic)

The schematic (Figure A-1, p. 18, TIPD118 Rev. A) and Figure 2 (p. 3) give: `C1 = 1 µF` and
`R1 = 100 kΩ` in series from `VIN` to the inverting input; `R2 = 1 kΩ` from the inverting input to
`VOUT`; `C2 = 1200 pF` from the inverting input to the **op-amp output** (pin 6); `R4 = 8.2 Ω`
from the op-amp output to `VOUT`; `C9 = 68 nF` from `VOUT` to ground; `R3 = 49.9 Ω` from `VIN` to
ground; the non-inverting input on `VCM`, held by `R5 = 1 kΩ` and `C10 = 0.1 µF` to ground (the
`VCM` jack open). With an ideal op amp this wiring gives exactly the guide's Eq. 2 (p. 4),
`VOUT/VIN = -(R2/R1) / (1 + (R2 + R4) C2 s + R2 R4 C2 C9 s²)`, so the low-pass pole `LPFPOLE2` of
Eq. 6 and Eq. 8 (p. 5) uses `C2`; the text of §2.1 (p. 3), which names `R2`, `R4` and `C9`, is
the inconsistent one. Two other discrepancies in TI's documents: the bill of materials (Figure A-2,
p. 19, line 2) lists `C2 = 1500 pF`, and the schematic labels `U1` as `OPA627AU` while the bill of
materials (line 19) and the guide (§3.1, p. 6) use the OPA1611. The supply decoupling (`C3`–`C8`,
`C11`, `C12`) is outside the linear model.

## The OPA1611 macromodel (SBOS450C, Rev. C, August 2014)

`G1` (1 mS) into `RP1 ∥ CP1`, a unit buffer `E1`, and the output impedance `RO1 + (LO1 ∥ RL1)`:

* `A0 = gm · RP1 = 3 162 278`: the open-loop gain 130 dB (typical, `RL = 10 kΩ`; §6.4, p. 6), as
  `10^6.5` rounded to 7 significant digits;
* `CP1 = 3.979 pF`: `gm / (2π CP1) = 39.9997 MHz`, the gain-bandwidth product 40 MHz (`G = 1`;
  §6.4, p. 5; also SLAU518 §3.1, p. 6), from `1 mS / (2π · 40 MHz) = 3.97887 pF` rounded to 4
  digits; the dominant pole is `1 / (2π RP1 CP1) = 12.65 Hz`;
* `RO1 = 0.722 Ω`, `LO1 = 893 nH`, `RL1 = 58.8 Ω`: a least-squares fit (log magnitude, rounded to
  3 digits) to the open-loop output impedance of Figure 28 (p. 11), read from the figure's vector
  path, over 60 kHz–40 MHz, where it is within 1.2 dB. Below the minimum of `|ZO|` near 60 kHz
  the figure rises like a capacitance (1.9 kΩ at 10 Hz), which the model omits.

`opa_transferFunction` checks the open-loop transfer function of the macromodel from its text:
from the differential input and from a test current into the output to the output voltage it is
`[A0 ωp / (s + ωp), RO1 + RL1 LO1 s / (LO1 s + RL1)]` with `ωp = 1 / (RP1 CP1)`.

## What is certified

* `opaText_check`, `opa_transferFunction`: the macromodel and its open-loop transfer function.
* `tipdText_check`: exponential stability of TIPD118 with the macromodel, and its input/output
  realization, from the text (`Cslib.Electrical.Text.exists_parse_of_checkText`).
* `tipd_decay`, `tipd_impulse_le`: the decay rate `α = 9 s⁻¹` of the state of the checked
  realization and the envelope `|h t|² ≤ γ β exp (-2 α t)` of the impulse response from `VIN` to
  `VOUT` (`γ β = 2555904 · 242`). The slowest closed-loop pole is the input high-pass
  `≈ -1/(R1 C1) = -10 s⁻¹`, so the rate measures the high-pass corner, not the margin of the
  capacitive-load loop.
* `tipd_range`: stability for every `C9 ∈ [1 aF, 10 µF]` (SLAU518 claims `C9 ≤ 100 nF`, §2.1),
  at the rate `α = 9 s⁻¹`, from a cover of the range by 21 intervals with one generalized
  Lyapunov certificate each (`Cslib.Electrical.CapacitanceRangeCert`); `tipdNoC9Text_check`: the
  circuit without `C9`.
* `tipd47_range`: with `R4 = 4.7 Ω`, stability for every `C9 ∈ [4.7 nF, 100 nF]`; below that
  range the model is numerically unstable between about `0.78 nF` and `3.35 nF`.
* `tipd100nText_check`, `tipd1uText_check`, `tipdBomText_check`: stability with `C9 = 100 nF` (the
  guide's upper limit) and `C9 = 1 µF` (both also covered by `tipd_range`), and with the bill of
  materials' `C2`.
* Rejections: the nominal certificate is not accepted for the circuit without `R4`, and for that
  circuit an honest decoupling with the (rounded) solution of `Aᵀ P + P A = -I` is rejected
  because `P` is indefinite. A rejection is not an instability proof; the instability of that
  circuit is numerical only here.

The closed-loop poles, the phase margin and the step overshoots that the pull request compares with
TI's claims are numerical, not certified.
-/

